# The rendered docs site and the built console app. Published by .github/workflows/
# pages.yaml, which now RENDERS them at deploy time instead of copying committed bytes.
#
# They were tracked for years, and the cost was not the disk: every page embeds the commit
# that last touched its source, so committing a source change immediately staled its own
# rendered output. The fix was a second "refresh generated metadata" commit after every
# real one, forever, because amending would change the hash the footer had just recorded.
# Rendering in CI knows the final commit already, so the cycle disappears.
#
# They are also 27% of this repository's blob history (17.6 MB docs/gen + 4.6 MB
# console/gen of 83.2 MB), which is the exact cost a CI checkout pays. Untracking stops
# the growth; it does not shrink what is already there - the blobless clone in
# docs/guides/integrations/ci.md is what keeps CI from downloading the old versions.
#
# docs/active.urls.lock stays TRACKED on purpose: it lives outside gen/ and is the ledger
# abandonedLinkCheck reads to prove a previously published URL never starts 404-ing. That
# gate only works if the ledger survives across builds.
# docs/vendor/playground/ stays tracked too: TinyGo builds the wasm and CI has none.
#
# `docs/gen/*` rather than `docs/gen/`, because git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# DIRECTORY is excluded - the trailing-slash form would make the negation below silently
# do nothing. Excluding the entries instead leaves docs/gen itself includable.
/docs/gen/*
# docs/gen/install is hand-authored, not rendered: it is the POSIX sh installer served at
# /magus/install, linted by the root shellcheck target, and nothing under docs/ writes it
# (engine/gates.buzz only lists it in pruneStaleOutputs' alwaysKeep so the render leaves it
# alone). It is a source file misfiled inside the output tree, and it stays tracked where it
# is because .github/actions/setup-magus sparse-checks-out this exact path from the default
# branch - moving it would break that action for every caller pinned to an older tag.
!/docs/gen/install
# docs/gen/public/release/index.json is the release manifest `magus self update`
# fetches, and index.json.sig is its detached Ed25519 signature. Both are tracked for
# the same reason: signing needs MAGUS_SIGNING_KEY, which only a v* tag build holds,
# while the site renders on every push to main - so a rendered index.json would
# overwrite the signed one and put the .sig back to 404ing. `magus-utils release-index`
# writes the pair at release time and release.yaml opens a PR carrying it.
#
# Four lines rather than two, because of the same rule the comment above notes: a
# negation cannot reach inside an excluded DIRECTORY, so every parent has to be
# re-included and then re-excluded on the way down.
!/docs/gen/public/
/docs/gen/public/*
!/docs/gen/public/release/
/docs/gen/public/release/*
!/docs/gen/public/release/index.json
!/docs/gen/public/release/index.json.sig
# The signed registry, tracked for the same reason: only a job holding
# MAGUS_REGISTRY_KEY can produce it, and the site render must copy it rather than
# rebuild it. registry.yaml opens the pull request that lands it.
!/docs/gen/public/registry/
/docs/gen/public/registry/*
!/docs/gen/public/registry/index.json
!/docs/gen/public/registry/index.json.sig
console/gen/

# The assembled deploy tree: `magus run deploy-generate .` writes it from the two gen/ trees
# and the Pages workflow uploads it. Building it locally is how you see what publishes.
# Under gen/ because that is what generated output is called in every project here; the
# root's own gen/*.json (the graph exports) stays tracked beside it.
/gen/site/

# Go coverage profile — throwaway intermediate from `magus run coverage`. The
# rendered coverage.svg badge is committed; the profile is not.
coverage.out
coverage.hand.out

# Local build artifacts: `go build ./cmd/<name>` at the repo root drops the bare
# basename here. /magus-* is a wildcard so a newly added cmd/magus-* tool is covered
# without editing this list (an explicit list once missed magus-spelldocs and swept
# the binary into a commit); the non-prefixed tools are named individually.
/magus
/magus-*
/coverage-badge
/langservice-manifest
/buzz-playground

# golangci-lint binary built from libs/testsprawl/.custom-gcl.yml, carrying the
# in-repo linters. Rebuilt from source, never committed.
/custom-gcl

# release output from `magus run release` — cross-compiled tarballs + SHA256SUMS,
# published to the GitHub release, never committed
/dist/

# buildx --metadata-file output from image-build, one per variant. Untracked and never
# committed: image-build is skip_cache, so nothing snapshots them, and left un-ignored
# they make the whole-tree vcs\isDirty check in generate read as already-dirty, which
# silently downgrades the drift gate to a no-op on the next run.
/static-meta.json
/cgo-meta.json
/snapshot-meta.json

# macOS directory metadata
.DS_Store

# magus runtime cache
.magus/

# SCIP code-symbol indexes are build artifacts (magus writes them under .magus, but a
# manual `scip-*` run can drop one in the tree); never commit them.
*.scip

# compiled Go test binaries (go test -c / bench runs)
*.test

# editor autosaves and backups. Not hypothetical: an emacs autosave (#action.yml#) was
# committed next to .github/actions/setup-magus/action.yml and had to be removed by hand.
# They sit beside the file they shadow, so they slip into a `git add -A` unnoticed.
\#*\#
.\#*
*~
*.orig
*.swp
*.swo

# Agent surface: GENERATED, not source.
#
# `magus agent install` writes these from the binary and stamps each with the schema and
# content hashes it was built from - so they are a rendering of cmd/magus/skills/, tracked in
# three provider trees at once. Committed, one `magus run build` triplicated 33 files into
# version control and every merge conflicted on output nobody edits. Regenerate them instead:
#   magus agent install .claude/skills --force
#
# magus-skill-authoring and magus-local are the exceptions and stay tracked: both are
# hand-authored, never embedded in the binary, and nothing regenerates them. Hand-authored
# stays, generated goes. magus-local is additionally the name magus RESERVES for a workspace's
# own rules (internal/agent.LocalSkillName, held by TestLocalSkillNameIsReserved), so install
# and verify leave it alone by construction - but only if it is committed does it survive a
# fresh clone or a new worktree.
.agents/skills/
.opencode/skills/
.claude/skills/*
!.claude/skills/magus-skill-authoring/
!.claude/skills/magus-local/

# Harness wiring, not repo configuration. settings.json hard-wires the PreToolUse guard to a
# `magus hook` binary that a fresh clone may not have built - when it resolves something stale,
# every Bash/Edit/Write fails closed for that contributor, which is how this file cost a whole
# session once. launch.json pins one machine's dev-server command and port. Both are per
# developer; the guard SCRIPTS they point at (docs/guides/integrations/agents/) are the
# artifact worth versioning.
.claude/settings.json
.claude/launch.json

# Fetched upstream responses the registry aggregator reads. Not committed: the
# published file records each response digest, which is what -verify compares
# against, and archiving several hundred raw responses is a cost this project has
# not priced. See docs/scope.md.
/upstream/
